{
  "result": "On the morning the laundromat closed, someone washed a single yellow curtain. It turned behind the round glass, rising and collapsing like a small weather system. Outside, a man unscrewed the sign. Every time his drill stopped, you could hear the curtain\u2019s brass rings tapping the drum.\n\nI had been coming there for eleven years and knew almost nothing about anyone. I knew that the woman who brought a book never turned a page until her wash began. I knew that a boy once fit entirely inside a laundry cart and, by the following spring, had to fold his knees. I knew that the owner kept a jar of loose buttons beside the register. If you found yours, she made you take a second one.\n\nThat morning she tipped the jar onto the folding table and invited us to help ourselves. I took a green button with a nick on its rim. It matched nothing I owned. The woman with the book picked out four white ones and laid them in a row before slipping them into her coat pocket.\n\nWhen the curtain was dry, its owner shook it out. A warm gust traveled the length of the folding table and stirred the paper sign that said EXACT CHANGE. Then she folded the curtain over her arm, and we all stood aside to let her through.\n\nI still have the button. Every few years I find it while looking for something else, and for a moment my hands smell clean.",
